from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "3. Healing in Israel’s Scriptures"),
("p", "Claim: in the law, the psalms and the prophets, healing is promised to a people under covenant, and it is set beside obedience and beside judgment. Individual healings are given on the word of a prophet and on a king’s prayer. The passages are addressed to Israel, the Lord is the healer in each, and none of them is addressed to a people made up of Jew and Gentile in one body. The chapter reads them in order and records what each states."),
("sub", "3.1 The LORD that healeth thee"),
("p", "The first statement of the LORD as healer comes at the first camp after the sea. “And said, If thou wilt diligently hearken to the voice of the LORD thy God, and wilt do that which is right in his sight, and wilt give ear to his commandments, and keep all his statutes, I will put none of these diseases upon thee, which I have brought upon the Egyptians: for I am the LORD that healeth thee” (Exodus 15:26). The speaker is the LORD, the hearer is Israel, and the sentence is conditional. The promise that none of the diseases of Egypt will be put on the people is tied to hearkening and to keeping the statutes, and the name is given as the reason: “I am the LORD that healeth thee.”"),
("p", "The same promise returns in the law. “And ye shall serve the LORD your God, and he shall bless thy bread, and thy water; and I will take sickness away from the midst of thee” (Exodus 23:25). And again: “And the LORD will take away from thee all sickness, and will put none of the evil diseases of Egypt, which thou knowest, upon thee; but will lay them upon all them that hate thee” (Deuteronomy 7:15). In each the promise is made to Israel at the covenant and each sets health beside the service of the LORD. The sentences state what the LORD will do for a nation that serves Him. They do not state what He will do for every person who is sick."),
("sub", "3.2 The other side of the covenant"),
("p", "The law that promises health also names sickness as a judgment on the nation. “But it shall come to pass, if thou wilt not hearken unto the voice of the LORD thy God, to observe to do all his commandments and his statutes which I command thee this day; that all these curses shall come upon thee, and overtake thee” (Deuteronomy 28:15). And: “The LORD shall make the pestilence cleave unto thee, until he have consumed thee from off the land, whither thou goest to possess it” (Deuteronomy 28:21). The list goes on to a consumption, a fever, an inflammation and “the botch of Egypt” “whereof thou canst not be healed” (Deuteronomy 28:22, 27). The blessing and the curse are two halves of one covenant with one people, and the passages that promise healing cannot be read without the passages that name disease."),
("p", "The law also states, in one sentence, who holds the power on both sides. “See now that I, even I, am he, and there is no god with me: I kill, and I make alive; I wound, and I heal: neither is there any that can deliver out of my hand” (Deuteronomy 32:39). The statement is made in Moses’ song to Israel. It is a statement of the LORD’s sovereignty over life and sickness, and the volume reads it as such. It does not turn it into a promise that every wound will be healed."),
("sub", "3.3 The psalmists and the prophets"),
("p", "The psalms speak of healing in praise. “Bless the LORD, O my soul, and forget not all his benefits: Who forgiveth all thine iniquities; who healeth all thy diseases” (Psalm 103:2, 3). Another psalm says of the LORD, “He sent his word, and healed them, and delivered them from their destructions” (Psalm 107:20). The sentences are written in the first person of a believing Israelite and in praise of the God of Israel for what He had done in the nation’s history and in the singer’s own life."),
("p", "The prophets use the word of the nation. “For I will restore health unto thee, and I will heal thee of thy wounds, saith the LORD; because they called thee an Outcast, saying, This is Zion, whom no man seeketh after” (Jeremiah 30:17). “Come, and let us return unto the LORD: for he hath torn, and he will heal us; he hath smitten, and he will bind us up” (Hosea 6:1). In Isaiah the promise is set in the restored land: “And the inhabitant shall not say, I am sick: the people that dwell therein shall be forgiven their iniquity” (Isaiah 33:24). And Malachi closes the prophets with the promise to those who fear the name: “But unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings” (Malachi 4:2)."),
("p", "In these passages healing is a promise made to a nation: to Zion, to the people returning, to the inhabitants of a restored land. The sickness named is a wound in the national life, and the healing is the nation’s restoration. The passages are not read here as a promise that every sick Israelite will recover, and they are not read as addressed to anyone outside Israel."),
("sub", "3.4 Naaman and Hezekiah"),
("p", "Two individual healings stand in the histories, and each is given a setting. Naaman, “captain of the host of the king of Syria,” “was a leper” (2 Kings 5:1). A captive girl said, “Would God my lord were with the prophet that is in Samaria! for he would recover him of his leprosy” (2 Kings 5:3). Elisha sent word: “Go and wash in Jordan seven times, and thy flesh shall come again to thee, and thou shalt be clean” (2 Kings 5:10). Naaman “dipped himself seven times in Jordan, according to the saying of the man of God: and his flesh came again like unto the flesh of a little child, and he was clean” (2 Kings 5:14). The Lord recalls it in the synagogue at Nazareth: “And many lepers were in Israel in the time of Eliseus the prophet; and none of them was cleansed, saving Naaman the Syrian” (Luke 4:27). The healing is given on the word of a prophet in Israel, to a Gentile, and the Lord names it as an instance of how few in Israel were cleansed."),
("p", "“In those days was Hezekiah sick unto death” (2 Kings 20:1). The prophet Isaiah told him, “Set thine house in order; for thou shalt die, and not live.” Hezekiah “turned his face to the wall, and prayed unto the LORD,” and the word of the LORD came again: “I have heard thy prayer, I have seen thy tears: behold, I will heal thee” (2 Kings 20:2, 5). Isaiah said, “Take a lump of figs. And they took and laid it on the boil, and he recovered” (2 Kings 20:7). Hezekiah asked, “What shall be the sign that the LORD will heal me?” and the shadow went back ten degrees on the dial of Ahaz (2 Kings 20:8–11). The healing is given to a king of Judah in answer to his prayer, by the word of a prophet, with a means applied and a sign given. All three are named in the text."),
("sub", "3.5 What the passages leave unsaid"),
("p", "The Scriptures of Israel do not make every sickness a judgment. Satan “smote Job with sore boils from the sole of his foot unto his crown,” and Job answered, “What? shall we receive good at the hand of God, and shall we not receive evil?” (Job 2:7, 10). They also record a king who sought the wrong help: “And Asa in the thirty and ninth year of his reign was diseased in his feet, until his disease was exceeding great: yet in his disease he sought not to the LORD, but to the physicians” (2 Chronicles 16:12). The sentence reports what Asa did and sets it beside his not seeking the LORD. It does not forbid physicians, and the same history records that Isaiah laid figs on Hezekiah’s boil."),
("p", "The passages leave several things unsaid. They do not say that a healing is owed to every believer who prays. They do not say that sickness always follows sin or that healing always follows faith. They do not address any person outside the nation, except that Naaman is healed in Israel by Israel’s prophet. The volume carries these silences into the chapters that follow."),
("tbl", "Healing in Israel’s Scriptures.", [
    ("Exodus 15:26", T("Exod 15:26"), "The LORD speaks to Israel; the promise is conditional on hearkening and keeping the statutes."),
    ("Deuteronomy 7:15", T("Deut 7:15"), "Health is promised to the nation; disease is laid on those that hate."),
    ("Psalm 103:2, 3", T("Ps 103:2, 3"), "An Israelite blesses the LORD who forgives and heals."),
    ("Jeremiah 30:17", T("Jer 30:17"), "Healing is promised to Zion, the outcast."),
    ("2 Kings 20:5", T("2 Kgs 20:5"), "A king’s prayer is heard and he is healed, with a means and a sign."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "In Israel’s Scriptures the LORD is the healer, and the promises of health are made to a nation under covenant.",
    "The promise of health stands beside the curse of disease in one covenant, and each is conditioned on the nation’s hearkening.",
    "The prophets use healing of the nation’s restoration in the land.",
    "Individual healings are given on a prophet’s word or a king’s prayer, with the means and the sign named.",
    "The passages do not say that every sick person is owed a healing, and they do not address any people outside Israel.",
  ]),
]
